@@s001 A fold written with the other
@@s002 dato:functions set aside,ora:computation starting now,ok:result
@@s003 One starts from the function that does nothing: <b>g0 = identity</b>. It is the "initial value" when the accumulator is a function.
@@s004 <b>g1</b> = "first do +1, then continue with g0". Nothing is computed yet: a computation is set aside.
@@s005 <b>g2</b> = "first do ×10, then continue with g1". Still nothing computed.
@@s006 Only now is it run: <code>g2(2)</code>. The last one added starts: 2 × 10 = <b>20</b>.
@@s007 g1(20) = g0(20 + 1) = g0(<b>21</b>).
@@s008 g0(21) = <b>21</b>. The calculation added last was run first: it is the trick that allows writing foldLeft with foldRight.
@@s009 ora:element,dato:accumulator,ok:result
@@s010 due:function built (no number computed),ok:run on z = 0
@@s011 no:error,ok:correct
@@s012 A fold written with the other
@@s013 Exercises 10 and 11 of week 2. The first is solved by reversing the list. The second, for the exercise sheet "perhaps the hardest of the course", requires a new idea: instead of accumulating a <b>value</b>, you accumulate a <b>function</b> and run it at the end.
@@s014 Exercise 10: foldRight with foldLeft
@@s015 foldRight combines starting from the <b>last</b> element, foldLeft from the <b>first</b>. If I reverse the list, the first of the reversed list is the last of the original.
@@s016 foldRight1
@@s017 The <code>f</code> I receive is the one of foldRight: it wants <b>(element, result)</b>.
@@s018 I reverse the list: so foldLeft will visit it from the last element to the first.
@@s019 But foldLeft calls its function with <b>(accumulator, element)</b>: the opposite order.
@@s020 So I pass it a small function that <b>swaps</b> the arguments before calling <code>f</code>. The name of the parameter does not matter: the position matters.
@@s021 foldRight1(List(1, 2, 3), 0, _ - _) must give 1 - (2 - (3 - 0)) = 2
@@s022 Reversed list.
@@s023 foldLeft takes the first (3) and the accumulator (0). The swap makes <code>f</code> receive them as (element, result).
@@s024 Then 2.
@@s025 Then 1. Result 2, equal to foldRight (checked).
@@s026 Advantage: it is in tail position, so it does not exhaust the stack on long lists. Disadvantage: it traverses the list twice.
@@s027 reverse + foldLeft with the arguments swapped
@@s028 Before exercise 11: a function as accumulator
@@s029 The new idea, on a small example. Instead of calculating right away, I note down <b>what to do</b>, composing functions. Then I run everything at once.
@@s030 Setting the calculations aside
@@s031 One starts from the function that does nothing: the <b>identity</b>. It is the "initial value" when I accumulate functions.
@@s032 I add a calculation: "first do +1, then continue with g0". I have calculated nothing.
@@s033 I add: "first do ×10, then continue with g1".
@@s034 Only now do I run. The calculation added <b>last</b> (×10) is run <b>first</b>.
@@s035 Functions as accumulator
@@s036 The key point
@@s037 Building the functions in one order, you run them in the <b>opposite</b> order. foldRight builds starting from the last element: if it accumulates functions, it will run them starting from the first. Which is exactly the order of foldLeft.
@@s038 Exercise 11: foldLeft with foldRight
@@s039 foldLeft1, piece by piece
@@s040 I call foldRight saying that its result is <b>not a B but a function B => B</b>. It is the hint of the exercise sheet.
@@s041 Initial value: the identity. For the empty list foldLeft must return <code>z</code> as it is, and the identity applied to <code>z</code> gives exactly <code>z</code>.
@@s042 The step function of foldRight receives an element <code>a</code> and the "result of the rest", which here is a function <code>g</code>: the function that knows how to handle all the elements after <code>a</code>.
@@s043 It produces a new function: given an accumulator <code>b</code>, it <b>first</b> combines <code>a</code> (as foldLeft would: <code>f(b, a)</code>), <b>then</b> passes the result to <code>g</code> for the rest.
@@s044 foldRight returns a function. I run it right away on <code>z</code>. Without this <code>(z)</code> I would return a function instead of a B.
@@s045 foldLeft1(List(1, 2, 3), 0, _ - _): must give ((0 - 1) - 2) - 3 = -6
@@s046 dato:identity and z,ora:element combined now,due:function already built,ok:result
@@s047 foldRight unrolls the list: <code>step</code> is <code>(a, g) => b => g(f(b, a))</code>, <code>id</code> is the identity. It is computed from the inside.
@@s048 Element 3: the function "subtract 3". Let's call it <code>g3</code>. No number calculated.
@@s049 Element 2: "subtract 2, then do g3". It is <code>g2</code>.
@@s050 Element 1: "subtract 1, then do g2". foldRight has finished and returns this function.
@@s051 The final <code>(z)</code>: I run it with <code>z = 0</code>.
@@s052 The brackets are on the left, as in foldLeft. Result −6 (checked). I built from the right and ran from the left.
@@s053 No cheating allowed
@@s054 The text says it: do not use <code>reverse</code> here. <code>reverse</code> is a special case of foldLeft, so using it would mean writing foldLeft using foldLeft.
@@s055 Building functions from the right, running them from the left
@@s056 Typical error
